diff --git a/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..82ac53c
--- /dev/null
+++ b/doc/Manual/ManualCreditControl.html
@@ -0,0 +1,73 @@
+<a id="CreditControl"><h1>Customer Credit Control</h1></a>
+<p>
+Credit control in webERP applies the same rules every time, by everybody, and leaves a trail the auditors can follow. It covers credit reviews, holding and releasing accounts, overdue reminders, late payment charges and the write-off of bad debt. The screens are on the Receivables menus. Only the credit controller and the deputy - the users whose security role holds the security token <i>Credit control - reviews, holds, reminders, charges and write-offs</i> (token 21) - can change anything. A new database gives the token to the System Administrator role; give it to the role of the credit controller in the Access Permissions Maintenance screen. Everyone else with access to customers can look at the credit status, the reviews and the history but not change them.
+</p>
+<p>
+Sales people who log in see credit information only for their own customers and their own transactions in every list and report described here. Customers who log in see their own reminders and charges only, on the screen <i>My Reminders and Charges</i>.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CreditReviews"><h2>Credit Reviews</h2></a>
+<p>
+Every trade customer is reviewed at least once a year - large or risky customers more often. Choose <i>Credit Reviews</i> on the Receivables Transactions menu or follow the link on the customer page. A review records the date, the user who is logged in, the outcome, the new limit where it changes, the date the next review is due and the comments. The comments can run to several paragraphs; quotes from the customer's emails can be pasted in. The outcome is one of:
+</p>
+<ul>
+<li>Limit kept</li>
+<li>Limit raised - enter the new, higher limit</li>
+<li>Limit lowered - enter the new, lower limit</li>
+<li>Account put on hold - choose the credit status to give it, it must be a status that disallows invoicing</li>
+<li>Account put on watch - staff are warned, but can carry on</li>
+<li>Account released from hold - choose a credit status that allows invoicing</li>
+</ul>
+<p>
+When a review changes the limit or the credit status the customer's account is changed in the same database transaction as the review is saved. If either part fails neither is saved. Comments must be entered whenever the limit or the status changes, so that the history always shows why. The next review date is set by the review (the default is the company setting <i>Credit Review Interval</i> months ahead) and is shown on the customer page; it can also be entered when a customer is created. A customer that has never been reviewed has no date and is listed as never reviewed in the report.
+</p>
+<p>
+A review entered by mistake can be corrected with the button <i>Entered by mistake</i> and a reason. The review stays in the history marked as a mistake, together with who corrected it and why, but it does not undo the change that was made to the account - record a new review to put the limit or the status right.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CreditHistory"><h2>Credit History</h2></a>
+<p>
+From the customer page, <i>Credit reviews, reminders and charges</i> shows the past reviews newest first, the reminders that have been sent with the invoices each included, the late payment charges and the bad debt write-offs of the customer.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CreditReviewReport"><h2>Credit Reviews Due Report</h2></a>
+<p>
+The report lists customers whose review is overdue, never done, or due within the number of days that you choose, with their balance, the overdue amount (invoices past due), the credit limit, the credit still available (the limit less the balance and the value of outstanding orders, as used at order entry) and the date they last paid. The report can be printed as PDF.
+</p>
+
+<a id="HoldReleaseAccounts"><h2>Holding and Releasing Accounts</h2></a>
+<p>
+Each customer has a credit status (see the section on credit status ratings). A status can allow invoicing, put the account on watch, or put it on hold. An account on hold gets no new invoices by any route: orders keyed by staff, recurring orders (the template is left untouched and is picked up again once the hold is released), electronic (EDI) orders, orders and invoices created through the web connection (API) and the invoicing of orders that were entered before the hold. An account on watch lets staff carry on but warns them. When an order would take a customer over the credit limit, what happens follows the company setting <i>Check Credit Limits</i> and nothing in credit control overrides it.
+</p>
+<p>
+Only the credit controller can put an account on hold, release it or change the limit; on the customer maintenance screen the credit limit, status and review date are shown but disabled to everyone else. Neither can the web connection change them.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CreditReminders"><h2>Overdue Reminders</h2></a>
+<p>
+There are three reminder levels: a friendly reminder once an invoice is past due, a firm reminder at the first overdue band and a final notice at the second overdue band. The bands are the number of days of the company settings <i>First Overdue Deadline</i> and <i>Second Overdue Deadline</i>, and "past due" means exactly what the invoice, the statement, the customer inquiry and the aged debtors report call past due. The wording of each letter is in the system parameters. Customers on hold get the final notice wording.
+</p>
+<p>
+On the screen <i>Overdue Reminders</i> you see who would get which level, you can untick anyone you want to leave out, and then you send. Each reminder is a PDF letter on the company letterhead emailed to the contacts of the customer who receive statements. If the customer has no such contact, or the email fails, the letter is not counted as sent and the customer is listed among the reminders not sent. A customer never gets the same level twice for the same invoices, even if the run is repeated: an invoice is reminded again only when it has reached a higher level. The reminders sent are on the customer's credit history page.
+</p>
+
+<a id="LateCharges"><h2>Late Payment Charges</h2></a>
+<p>
+At month end the credit controller may charge interest on the invoices that are past due at the rate per month set in the system parameters, and a fixed reminder fee for each final notice sent that month (a letter with the final notice wording, which includes the ones to customers on hold). Nothing is charged until you tick the charges and press the button. The charges are customer transactions in the currency of the customer: they appear on the account, the inquiry and the statement, count in the aged debtors, can be printed and emailed like an invoice and can be matched against payments in the allocation screen. They are posted to the ledger in the same period - debit debtors control and credit the late payment charges income account chosen in the company preferences - and can carry GL tags. Running the charges again for the same month cannot charge twice.
+</p>
+
+<a id="BadDebtWriteOff"><h2>Bad Debt Write-off</h2></a>
+<p>
+Choose the customer, tick the invoices (or late payment charges) with an amount left to pay - the box in the heading selects the whole balance - and enter the reason. The write-off clears the items it covers. It is posted to the bad debt expense account chosen in the company preferences, which can never be a control account, and credits debtors control. For a customer in a foreign currency the difference between the rate of the invoice and the rate of the write-off goes to the sales exchange variances account, the same way as for a payment. The write-off is a customer transaction like the late payment charges.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CustomerCreditNotices"><h2>My Reminders and Charges</h2></a>
+<p>
+A customer who logs in sees the reminders and the late payment charges addressed to the customer, and nobody else's.
+</p>
+
+<a id="CreditControlSettings"><h2>Settings</h2></a>
+<p>
+The system parameters hold the credit review interval in months, the late payment interest rate per month, the reminder fee and the wording of the three reminder letters. The company preferences hold the two new ledger accounts, the late payment charges income account and the bad debt expense account; they cannot be deleted in the chart of accounts while they are in use there. A new installation, with or without demo data, has these settings; an existing database gets them from the database upgrade, which runs by itself from the Database Upgrade screen after the new code is installed.
+</p>
+<!-- Help End: CreditControl -->
